\ ff-test: lines
\ With FF_SAFE_MEM, every word that takes an address, a string or an
\ execution token checks it first. These words used to read or call
\ through whatever number was on the stack: `?`, `type`, `dump`, `find`,
\ `>name` and `>body`, and `evaluate` and `strlen` read on past the end
\ of a string without a terminator. And a program could write into
\ bytecode. Runs only in FF_SAFE_MEM builds; the file words are checked
\ in 037_memsafe_files.

: p 32 emit . ;

0 ?
12345 >name
12345 >body
0 type
0 find
0 100 dump
0 evaluate
0 strlen
clear

\ A cell with no NUL in it isn't a string, however it is reached.
variable v 0x4141414141414141 v !
v type
v strlen
"abc" v strcmp
v evaluate
clear

\ Bytecode can be read but not written: storing into a colon definition,
\ appending to one with `,` - the word being compiled included - or
\ copying over a string compiled into one could forge what the inner
\ interpreter follows.
: code-word 1 2 + ;
' code-word >body @ 0<> p cr
' code-word >body 0 swap !
: forged [ 5 , ] 1 ;
forged
7 ,
: literal-word "abc" ;
literal-word "xyz" swap s!
literal-word "x" swap s+
literal-word type cr
clear
create data-word 3 allot
7 data-word ! data-word @ p cr

\ Valid arguments pass. `>name` and `strerror` hand out copies that the
\ checks know about.
' dup >name type cr
"dup" find ' dup = p cr
"abc" v s! v type cr
2 strerror strlen 0> p cr
